--- a/CHANGELOG.md
+++ b/CHANGELOG.md
@@ -2,6 +2,7 @@
 
 ## Unreleased
 
+- C++ member calls resolve through the receiver's type: `e.start()` with a local `Engine e;` (also `Engine e(3);`, `auto e = Engine();`, `auto* e = new Engine;`), a parameter `Pump* p` or `const Pump& p`, `auto p = std::make_unique<Pump>();`, a `std::unique_ptr<Pump>` or `shared_ptr` variable, a cast, a global, a member of the class the method is in (`engine.start()` in `Car::go`, `this->pump->start()`) or of a typed struct (`car.engine.start()`) is certain and reaches that class's method only; the out-of-line definition stands for its in-class declaration. A receiver whose class cannot be told (`auto x = lookup()`, a method inherited from a base class, an external type) stays a guess by name. Schema 25; C++ files re-parse once.
 - `callers` of a C function-pointer member lists the lines storing a function in it (`.irq_mask = foo_mask,`) as their own group after the call sites, `stores a function in it (not calls): N`, no longer among the references that are not calls, and they no longer make the answer read as incomplete. `callers` of a function counts the documentation lines naming it after the verdict (`plus N in docs (not code, not listed)`) and never lists them.
 - The SubagentStart brief no longer carries the swarm board protocol line by default: in A/B runs it gave parallel subagents nothing and cost more. `[hooks] swarm_board = true` in `config.toml` brings it back. The parent packet and parent read lines stay.
 
